{"name":"Beef, salt and water diet","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/DoughnDolce_Beef_Sandwich.jpg/960px-DoughnDolce_Beef_Sandwich.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DoughnDolce_Beef_Sandwich.jpg","caption":"DoughnDolce Beef Sandwich","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":21,"casual":{"lead":["The beef, salt and water diet, often called the lion diet, limits intake to ruminant meat, mostly beef, salt and water. Mikhaila Peterson created it in 2017 as an elimination protocol, and she and her father Jordan Peterson publicly followed it, after which it spread on social media. Followers promote it for leanness, clearer skin and better mood.","Advocates say removing all plant foods and most other animal foods removes potential triggers, and near-zero carbohydrate intake means the body runs on fat and protein, usually in ketosis. Accounts are mainly self-reports, and no controlled trials of this diet exist."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People eat beef or other ruminant meat, salt and water for a set stretch, often as an elimination period, and may later reintroduce other foods. Reported early fatigue and cramps usually fade within weeks."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Critics cite missing fibre and micronutrients, raised LDL cholesterol, constipation and, with high red-meat intake, associations with colon cancer and gout. Scurvy has been documented in a meat-only case. Dietitians' bodies classify carnivore diets as fad diets and flag nutrient gaps."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/DoughnDolce_Beef_Sandwich.jpg/960px-DoughnDolce_Beef_Sandwich.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/99/DoughnDolce_Beef_Sandwich.jpg?utm_source=com